// Every query of the video, in the order it appears.
// Where to run them: Eventhouse -> your KQL database -> the queryset (the item named <database>_queryset).
// Put the cursor inside one query (queries are separated by an empty line) and click Run.
// The table is OrdersLive: one row per order, filled by an Eventstream (see ../14-eventstream-basics).

// 1. A few rows, any rows: what does the table look like?
OrdersLive
| take 10

// 2. How many rows?
OrdersLive
| count

// 3. Some of the rows (where), some of the columns (project)
OrdersLive
| where Region == "Europe"
| project OrderTime, Product, City, SalesAmount

// 4. Group: orders and sales by region, biggest first
OrdersLive
| summarize Orders = count(), Sales = sum(SalesAmount) by Region
| order by Sales desc

// 5. The last five minutes ... this one FAILS on purpose:
//    "Cannot compare values of types string and datetime". OrderTime arrived as text.
OrdersLive
| where OrderTime > ago(5m)
| count

// 6. The fix: turn the text into a real date first
OrdersLive
| extend OrderTime = todatetime(OrderTime)
| where OrderTime > ago(5m)
| count

// 7. Save the fix once, as a function (a command: it begins with a dot and changes the database).
//    The empty line under this note matters: a command has to be the first line of its own block.

.create-or-alter function Orders() {
    OrdersLive
    | extend OrderTime = todatetime(OrderTime)
}

// 8. The same question, the short way. Run it twice: the window moves.
Orders
| where OrderTime > ago(5m)
| count

// 9. A week of orders by the hour, as a chart
Orders
| where OrderTime > ago(7d)
| summarize Orders = count() by bin(OrderTime, 1h)
| render timechart

// 10. The same, one line per region
Orders
| where OrderTime > ago(3d)
| summarize Orders = count() by bin(OrderTime, 1h), Region
| render timechart

// 11. The five products with the most sales in the last day
Orders
| where OrderTime > ago(1d)
| summarize Sales = sum(SalesAmount) by Product
| top 5 by Sales
| render barchart

// 12. What Copilot wrote for: "Using the Orders function, show the number of orders per city in the last hour, highest first"
Orders
| where OrderTime > ago(1h)
| summarize OrderCount = count() by City
| order by OrderCount desc

// 13. The retention policy as a command (the video shows it in the database details instead)

.show table OrdersLive policy retention
